(INTRODUCTION.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Vagabonds and Beggars are ancient blots in the history of the world.) Tj T*
(Idleness, I suppose, existed before civilization began, but feigned) Tj T*
(distress must certainly have been practised soon after.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the records of the Middle Ages enactments for the suppression and) Tj T*
(ordering of vagrancy continually occur. In this country, as we shall) Tj T*
(see directly, laws for its abolishment were passed at a very early date.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The begging system of the Friars, perhaps more than any other) Tj T*
(cause, contributed to swell the ranks of vagabonds. These religious) Tj T*
(mendicants, who had long been increasing in number and dissoluteness,) Tj T*
(gave to beggars sundry lessons in hypocrisy, and taught them, in) Tj T*
(their tales of fictitious distress, how to blend the troubles of the) Tj T*
(soul with the infirmities of the body. Numerous systems of religious) Tj T*
(imposture were soon contrived, and mendicants of a hundred orders) Tj T*
(swarmed through the land. Things were at their worst, or rather both) Tj T*
(friars and vagabonds were in their palmiest days, towards the latter) Tj T*
(part of the fifteenth century, just before the suppression of the) Tj T*
ET
