(Religious Houses commenced, and immediately before the first symptoms) Tj T*
(of the Reformation showed themselves,?that great movement which was so) Tj T*
(soon to sweep one of the two pests away for ever.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In Schreiber?s account of the _Bettler-industrie_ \(begging practices\)) Tj T*
(of Germany in the year 1475, he thus speaks of this golden age for) Tj T*
(mendicants.[1] His theory, as to the origin of the complicated system) Tj T*
(of mendicity, is, perhaps, more fanciful than true, but his account is) Tj T*
(nevertheless very interesting, and well worth extracting from.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?The beggars of Germany rejoiced in their Golden Age; it extended) Tj T*
(throughout nearly two centuries, from the invasions of the Turks until) Tj T*
(after the conclusion of the Swedish war \(1450 to 1650\). During this) Tj T*
(long period it was frequently the case that begging was practised less) Tj T*
(from necessity than for pleasure;?indeed, it was pursued like a regular) Tj T*
(calling. For poetry had estranged herself from the Nobility; knights) Tj T*
(no longer went out on adventures to seek giants and dragons, or to) Tj T*
(liberate the Holy Tomb; she had likewise become more and more alien to) Tj T*
(the Citizen, since he considered it unwise to brood over verses and) Tj T*
(rhymes, when he was called upon to calculate his profits in hard coin.) Tj T*
(Even the ?Sons of the Muses,? the Scholars, had become more prosaic,) Tj T*
(since there was so much to learn and so many universities to visit, and) Tj T*
(the masters could no longer wander from one country to another with) Tj T*
(thousands of pupils.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Then poetry \(as everything in human life gradually descends\) began) Tj T*
(to ally herself with beggars and vagrants. That which formerly had) Tj T*
(been misfortune and misery became soon a sort of free art, which only) Tj T*
(retained the mask of misery in order to pursue its course more safely) Tj T*
(and undisturbed. Mendicity became a distinct institution, was divided) Tj T*
(into various branches, and was provided with a language of its own.) Tj T*
(Doubtless, besides the frequent wars, it was the Gipsies?appearing in) Tj T*
(Germany, at the beginning of the fifteenth century, in larger swarms) Tj T*
(than ever?who contributed greatly to this state of things. They formed) Tj T*
(entire tribes of wanderers, as free as the birds in the air, now) Tj T*
(dispersing themselves, now reuniting, resting whereever forests or) Tj T*
(moors pleased, or stupidity and superstition allured them, possessing) Tj T*
(nothing, but appropriating to themselves the property of everybody, by) Tj T*
(stratagem or rude force.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?In what manner and to what extent such beggary had grown up and) Tj T*
(branched off towards the close of the fifteenth century, what) Tj T*
(artifices and even what language these beggars used to employ, is shown) Tj T*
(us in Johann Knebel?s Chronicles, the MSS. of which are preserved in) Tj T*
(the Library of the City and University of Bâle.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These MSS. are very curious. They contain the proceedings of the Trials) Tj T*
(at Basle,[2] in Switzerland, in 1475, when a great number of vagabonds,) Tj T*
(strollers, blind men, and mendicants of all orders, were arrested and) Tj T*
(examined. Johann Knebel was the chaplain of the cathedral there, and) Tj T*
(wrote them down at the time. From the reports of these trials it is) Tj T*
(believed the _Liber Vagatorum_ was compiled; and it is also conjectured) Tj T*
ET
