() Tj T*
(Knebel gives a long list of the different orders of beggars, and the) Tj T*
(names they were known by amongst themselves. This account is similar) Tj T*
(to, only not so spirited as that given in the _Liber Vagatorum_. The) Tj T*
(tricks and impostures are very nearly the same, together with the) Tj T*
(cant terms for the various tribes of mendicants. Knebel, speaking of) Tj T*
(the manner in which the tricks of these rogues were first found out,) Tj T*
(says:??At those times a great number of knaves went about the country) Tj T*
(begging and annoying people. Of these several were caught, and they) Tj T*
(told how they and their fellow-knaves were known, and when and how they) Tj T*
(used to meet, what they were called, and they told also several of) Tj T*
(their cant words.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The _Liber Vagatorum_, or _The Book of Vagabonds_, was probably written) Tj T*
(shortly after 1509, that year being mentioned in the work; it is the) Tj T*
(earliest book on beggars and their secret language of which we have any) Tj T*
(record,?preceding by half a century any similar work issued in this) Tj T*
(country.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Nothing is known of the author other than that it was written by) Tj T*
(one who styled himself a ?Reverend Magister, nomine expertus in) Tj T*
(truffis,??which proficiency in roguery, as Luther remarks, ?the little) Tj T*
(book very well proves, even though he had not given himself such a) Tj T*
(name.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(None of the early impressions bears a date, but the first edition is) Tj T*
(known to have been printed at Augsburg, about the year 1512-14, by) Tj T*
(Erhart Öglin, or Ocellus.[4] It is a small quarto, consisting of 12) Tj T*
(leaves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The title:?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  +Liber Vagatorum;) Tj T*
(  Der Betler Orden:+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(is printed in red. The title-page of this, as of most of the early) Tj T*
(editions, is embellished with a woodcut,?a facsimile of which is) Tj T*
(given in this translation. The picture, representing a beggar and) Tj T*
(his family, explains itself. At the foot of the title is printed, in) Tj T*
(black:?_Getrucht zu Augspurg durch Erhart Öglin_. The little book) Tj T*
(was frequently reprinted without any other variations than printers?) Tj T*
(blunders \(one edition having an error in the first word, _Lieber) Tj T*
