() Tj T*
(I should state, however, before proceeding further, that a metrical) Tj T*
(version of the _Liber Vagatorum_, in 838 verses, appeared about) Tj T*
(1517-18, written by Pamphilus Gengenbach, including a vocabulary of the) Tj T*
(beggars? cant. Although Karl Gödecke, in his work, _Ein Beitrag zur) Tj T*
(Deutschen Literatur Geschichte der Reformations zeit_ \(Hannover, Carl) Tj T*
(Rümpler, 1855\), has stated that Gengenbach?s poetical version preceded) Tj T*
(the smaller prose account, it is impossible, upon examining the two) Tj T*
(publications, to agree with him on this point. Gengenbach?s book) Tj T*
(certainly did not appear till after 1517, and the direct copies from) Tj T*
(the _Liber Vagatorum_, in matter and manner, are too frequent to admit) Tj T*
(for one moment of the supposition of their being accidental. The cant) Tj T*
(terms, too, are incorrectly given, and altogether the work bears the) Tj T*
(appearance of hasty and piratical compilation. It never met with that) Tj T*
(popularity which the author anticipated, and probably never crossed the) Tj T*
(frontiers of Switzerland.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The latest prose edition of the _Liber Vagatorum_ was issued towards) Tj T*
(the close of the seventeenth century. The title ran:?_Expertus in) Tj T*
(truffis. Of False Beggars and their knaveries. A pretty little book,) Tj T*
(made more than a century and a half since, together with a Vocabulary) Tj T*
(of some old cant words that occur therein, newly edited._ Anno 1668) Tj T*
(\(12^o. pp. 160\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That Luther should have written a Preface to so undignified a) Tj T*
(little work as _The Book of Vagabonds_ seems remarkable. At this) Tj T*
(period \(1528-9\) he was in the midst of his labours, surrounded with) Tj T*
(difficulties and cares, and with every moment of his time fully) Tj T*
(occupied. The Protest of Spires had just been signed by the first) Tj T*
(Protestants. Melancthon, in great affliction at the turbulent state of) Tj T*
(affairs, was running from city to city; and all Germany was alarmed) Tj T*
(to hear that the dreaded Turks were preparing to make battle before) Tj T*
(Vienna. Yet, the centre of all this agitation, engaged in directing and) Tj T*
(assisting his followers, Luther found time to write several popular) Tj T*
(pieces, and kept, we are told, the book-hawkers of Augsburg and Spires) Tj T*
(busy in supplying them to the people. These Christian pamphlets,) Tj T*
(D?Aubigné informs us, were eagerly sought for and passed through) Tj T*
(numberless editions. It was not the peasants and townspeople only who) Tj T*
(read them, but nobles and princes. Luther intended that they should) Tj T*
(be popular. He knew better than any man of his time how to captivate) Tj T*
(the reader and fix his attention. His little books were short, easy to) Tj T*
(read, full of homely sayings and current phrases, and ornamented with) Tj T*
(curious engravings. They were generally written, too, in Latin and) Tj T*
(German, to suit both the educated and the unlettered. One was entitled,) Tj T*
(_The Papacy with its Members painted and described by Dr. Luther_. In) Tj T*
