(+Of the Stabülers, or Bread Gatherers.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The next chapter is about the STABÜLERS. These are vagrants who tramp) Tj T*
(through the country from one Saint to another, their wives \(KRÖNERIN\)) Tj T*
(and children \(GATZAM\) going \(ALCHEN\) with them. Their hats \(WETTERHAN\)) Tj T*
(and cloaks \(WINTFANG\) hang full of signs of all the saints,?the cloak) Tj T*
(\(wintfang\) being made \(VETZEN\) out of a hundred pieces. They go to) Tj T*
(the peasants who give them bread \(LEHEM DIPPEN\); and each of these) Tj T*
(STABÜLERS has six or seven sacks, and carries a pot, plate, spoon,) Tj T*
(flask, and whatever else is needed for the journey with him. These) Tj T*
(same STABÜLERS never leave off begging, nor do their children, from) Tj T*
(their infancy to the day of their death?for the beggar?s staff keeps) Tj T*
(the fingers \(GRIFFLING\) warm?and they neither will nor can work, and) Tj T*
(their children \(GATZAM\) grow up to be harlots and harlotmongers \(GLIDEN) Tj T*
(und GLIDESVETZER\), hangmen and flayers \(ZWICKMEN und KAVELLER\). Also,) Tj T*
(whithersoever these STABÜLERS come, in town or country, they beg; at) Tj T*
(one house for God?s sake, at another for St. Valentine?s sake, at a) Tj T*
(third for St. Kürine?s, _sic de aliis_, according to the disposition) Tj T*
(of the people from whom they seek alms. For they do not adhere to one) Tj T*
(patron or trust to one method alone.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Conclusio_: Thou mayest give to them if thou wilt, for they are half) Tj T*
(bad and half good,?not all bad, but most part.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Lossners,[11] or liberated Prisoners.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The iij^{rd} chapter is about the LOSSNERS. These are knaves who say) Tj T*
(they have lain in prison vi or vij years, and carry the chains with) Tj T*
(them wherein they lay as captives among the infidel \(_id est_, in the) Tj T*
(SONNENBOSS, _i.e._ brothel\) for their christian faith; _item_, on the) Tj T*
(sea in galleys or ships enchained in iron fetters; _item_, in a strong) Tj T*
(tower for innocence? sake; and they have forged letters \(LOE BSAFFOT\),) Tj T*
(as from the princes and lords of foreign lands, and from the towns) Tj T*
(\(KIELAM\) there, to bear witness to their truth, tho? all the time) Tj T*
(they are deceit and lies \(GEVOPT und GEVERBT\),?? for vagabonds may) Tj T*
(be found everywhere on the road who can make \(VETZEN\) any seal they) Tj T*
(like?? and they say they have vowed to Our Lady at Einsiedlin \(in the) Tj T*
(DALLINGER?S BOSS, _i.e._ harlot?s house\), or to some other Saint \(in) Tj T*
(the SCHÖCHERBOSS, _i.e._ beer-house\), according to what country they) Tj T*
(are in, a pound of wax, a silver crucifix, or a chasuble; and they say) Tj T*
(they have been made free through that vow, and, when they had vowed,) Tj T*
(the chains opened and broke, and they departed safe and without harm.) Tj T*
(_Item_, some carry iron fastenings, or coats of mail \(PANZER\) with) Tj T*
(them, _et sic de aliis_. _Nota:_ They have perchance bought \(KÜMMERT\)) Tj T*
(the chains; perchance they had them made \(VETZEN\); perchance stolen) Tj T*
(\(GEJENFT\) them from the church \(DIFTEL\) of St. Lenhart.) Tj T*
