(+Of the Klenkners, or Cripples.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The iiij^{th} is about the KLENKNERS. These are the beggars who sit) Tj T*
(at the church-doors, and attend fairs and church gatherings with sore) Tj T*
(and broken legs; one has no foot, another no shank, a third no hand or) Tj T*
(arm. _Item_, some have chains lying by them, saying they have lain in) Tj T*
(captivity for innocence? sake, and commonly they have a St. Sebastianum) Tj T*
(or St. Lenhartum with them, and they pray and cry with a loud voice and) Tj T*
(noisy lamentations for the sake of the Saints, and every third word one) Tj T*
(of them speaks \(BARL\) is a lie \(GEVOP\), and the people who give alms) Tj T*
(to him are cheated \(BESEFELT\),?inasmuch as his thigh or his foot has) Tj T*
(rotted away in prison or in the stocks for wicked deeds. _Item_, one?s) Tj T*
(hand has been chopped off in the quarrels over dice or for the sake) Tj T*
(of a harlot. _Item_, many a one ties a leg up or besmears an arm with) Tj T*
(salves, or walks on crutches, and all the while as little ails him as) Tj T*
(other men. _Item_, at Utenheim there was a priest by name Master Hans) Tj T*
(Ziegler \(he holds now the benefice of Rosheim\), and he had his niece) Tj T*
(with him. One upon crutches came before his house. His niece carried) Tj T*
(him a piece of bread. He said, ?Wilt thou give me nought else?? She) Tj T*
(said, ?I have nought else.? He replied, ?Thou old priest?s harlot! wilt) Tj T*
(thou make thy parson rich?? and swore many oaths as big as he could) Tj T*
(utter them. She cried and came into the room and told the priest. The) Tj T*
(priest went out and ran after him. The beggar dropped his crutches) Tj T*
(and fled so fast that the parson could not catch him. A short time) Tj T*
(afterwards the parson?s house was burnt down; he said the KLENKNER did) Tj T*
(it. _Item_, another true example: at Schletstat, one was sitting at) Tj T*
(the church-door. This man had cut the leg of a thief from the gallows.) Tj T*
(He put on the dead leg and tied his own leg up. He had a quarrel with) Tj T*
(another beggar. This latter one ran off and told the townserjeant.) Tj T*
(When he saw the serjeant coming he fled and left the sore leg behind) Tj T*
(him and ran out of the town?a horse could hardly have overtaken him.) Tj T*
(Soon afterwards he hung on the gallows at Achern, and the dry leg) Tj T*
(beside him, and they called him Peter of Kreuzenach. _Item_, they are) Tj T*
(the biggest blasphemers thou canst find who do such things; and they) Tj T*
(have also the finest harlots \(GLIDEN\), they are the first-comers at) Tj T*
(fairs and church-celebrations, and the last-goers therefrom.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Conclusio_: Give them a kick on their hind parts if thou canst, for) Tj T*
(they are nought but cheats \(BESEFLER\) of the peasants \(HANZEN\) and all) Tj T*
(other men.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Example: One was called Uz of Lindau. He was at Ulm, in the hospital) Tj T*
(there, for xiiij days, and on St. Sebastian?s day he lay before a) Tj T*
(church, his hands and thighs tied up, nevertheless he could use both) Tj T*
(legs and hands. This was betrayed to the constables. When he saw them) Tj T*
(coming he fled from the town,?a horse could hardly have ran faster.) Tj T*
