(blind who have lost their sight by evil-doings and wickednesses. They) Tj T*
(wander about in the country and carry with them pictures of devils, and) Tj T*
(repair to the churches, and pretend they had been at Rome, to Saint) Tj T*
(James, and other distant places, and speak of great signs and wonders) Tj T*
(that had taken place, but it is all lies and deception. Some of the) Tj T*
(blind men are called BROKEN WANDERERS \(Bruch Umbgeen\). These are such) Tj T*
(as have been blinded ten years or more; they take cotton, and make the) Tj T*
(cotton bloody, and then with a kerchief tie this over their eyes, and) Tj T*
(say that they have been mercers or pedlers, and were blinded by wicked) Tj T*
(men in a forest, that they were tied fast to a tree and so remained) Tj T*
(three or four days, and, but for a merciful passer-by, they would have) Tj T*
(miserably perished;?and this is called BROKEN WANDERING.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Conclusio_: Know them well before thou givest to them; my advice is) Tj T*
(only give to those thou knowest.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Schwanfelders, Blickschlahers, or Naked Beggars.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xij^{th} chapter is about the SCHWANFELDERS, or BLICKSCHLAHERS.) Tj T*
(These are beggars who, when they come to a town, leave their clothes) Tj T*
(at the hostelry, and sit down against the churches naked, and shiver) Tj T*
(terribly before the people that they may think they are suffering from) Tj T*
(great cold. They prick themselves with nettle-feed and other things,) Tj T*
(whereby they are made to shake. Some say they have been robbed by) Tj T*
(wicked men; some that they have lain ill and for this reason were) Tj T*
(compelled to sell their clothes. Some say they have been stolen from) Tj T*
(them; but all this is only that people should give them more clothes,) Tj T*
(when they sell \(VERKÜMMERN\) them, and spend the money with lewd women) Tj T*
(\(VERBOLENS\) and gambling \(VERJONENS\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Conclusio_: Beware of these SCHWANFELDERS for it is all knavery, and) Tj T*
(give them nothing, whether they be men or women, \(unless\) thou knowest) Tj T*
(them well.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Voppers, or Demoniacs.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xiij^{th} chapter is about the VOPPERS. These beggars are for) Tj T*
(the most part women, who allow themselves to be led in chains as if) Tj T*
(they were raving mad; they tear their shifts from their bodies, in) Tj T*
(order that they may deceive people. There are also some that do both,) Tj T*
(VOPPERY and DUTZING, together. This is VOPPING, viz. when one begs for) Tj T*
(his wife?s or any other person?s sake and says she has been possessed) Tj T*
(of a devil \(tho? there is no truth in it\), and he has vowed to some) Tj T*
(Saint \(whom he names\), and must have xij pounds of wax or other things) Tj T*
(whereby the person will be delivered from the power of the devil. These) Tj T*
(are called DUTZING-VOPPERS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
ET
