(_Conclusio_: This is a wicked and false way of begging. They sing,?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  A beggar?s \(BREGAR\) wench \(ERLATIN\) will cheat,) Tj T*
(  And lie \(VOPPEN\) and be full of deceit \(FERBEN\):) Tj T*
(  And he kicks and beats her with his shoe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are also some VOPPERINAE, _id est_, women, who pretend that) Tj T*
(they have diseases of the breast. They take a cow?s spleen, and peel) Tj T*
(it on one side, and then lay it upon their bosom?the peeled part) Tj T*
(outside?besmearing it with blood, in order that people may think it is) Tj T*
(the breast. These are the VOPPERINAE.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Dallingers, or Hangmen.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xiiij^{th} chapter is about the D a l l i n g e r s. These are) Tj T*
(they who stand before the churches, having been hangmen \(although) Tj T*
(they have left it off i year or ii since\), and chastise and whip) Tj T*
(themselves with rods, and will do penance and pilgrimage for their sin) Tj T*
(and wickednesses. These often beg with much success. When they have) Tj T*
(practised for a while and cheated many people thereby, they become) Tj T*
(hangmen again, as before. Give to them if thou wilt; but they are all) Tj T*
(knaves who beg thus.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Dützbetterins, or Lying-in Women.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xv^{th} chapter is about the DÜTZBETTERINS. These are the) Tj T*
(beggarwomen who lay themselves before the churches all over the) Tj T*
(country. They spread a sheet over themselves, and set wax and eggs) Tj T*
(by them, as tho? they were in childbed, and say, their babe died) Tj T*
(xiiij days ago, altho? some of them have not had one these x or xx) Tj T*
(years; and they are called DÜTZBETTERINS. To these nothing is to be) Tj T*
(given,?_causa_: There lay once, at Strasburg, a man underneath a sheet) Tj T*
(before the cathedral, and it was pretended he was a woman in childbed.) Tj T*
(But he was taken by the town serjeants, and put into a halsong, and in) Tj T*
(the pillory, and then he was forbidden the country. There are likewise) Tj T*
(some women who pretend they have been pregnant with a monster and have) Tj T*
(brought forth such, as did a woman who came to Pforzheim in the year) Tj T*
(one thousand five hundred and nine. This same woman said that a short) Tj T*
(time before she had given birth to a child and a live toad; and that) Tj T*
(this very toad she had carried to Our Lady at Einsiedeln, where it was) Tj T*
(still alive, and that it must have a pound of meat every day,?being) Tj T*
(kept at Einsiedeln as a miracle. Thus she begs alms as if she were) Tj T*
(on her way to Ach, to Our Lady. She had also a letter with a seal,) Tj T*
(which was proclaimed from the pulpit. The same woman, however, had a) Tj T*
(lusty young man whom she kept in food by such villany, sitting in an) Tj T*
(ale-house in the suburb waiting for her. All this was found out by the) Tj T*
(gate-keeper; and they would have been seized, but they had been warned) Tj T*
(and so took themselves off. _Nota_: All this was utter knavery.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
