(+Of the Süntvegers, or \(pretended\) Murderers.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xvi^{th} chapter is about the SÜNTVEGERS. These are strong fellows) Tj T*
(who go about the country with long knives and say they have taken a) Tj T*
(man?s life away, but that it was in self-defence, and then they name a) Tj T*
(sum of money which they must have, and unless they bring the money at) Tj T*
(the right time, they will have their heads cut off. _Item_, some are) Tj T*
(accompanied by a fellow on their begging-rounds who goes in iron chains) Tj T*
(and fetters fastened with rings, and who says he was bail for the other) Tj T*
(for a sum of money to the people, and if he gets not the money in time,) Tj T*
(both of them must perish.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the female Süntvegers.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xvij^{th} chapter is about the FEMALE-SÜNTVEGERS. These are the) Tj T*
(wives \(KRÖNERIN\), or, in reality, the wenches \(GLIDEN\) of the above) Tj T*
(fellows \(_supra in precedenti capitulo_\). They wander over the country,) Tj T*
(and say that formerly they led a loose life, but that now they repent) Tj T*
(and would turn from their wickedness, and beg alms for the sake of) Tj T*
(Sancta Maria Magdalena, and cheat the people therewith.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Bil-wearers,[13] or \(pretended\) pregnant Women.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xviij^{th} chapter is about the BIL-WEARERS. These are the women) Tj T*
(who tie old jerkins, or clothes, or a pillow over their person,) Tj T*
(underneath the gown, in order that people may think they are with) Tj T*
(child; and they have not had one for xx years or more. This is called) Tj T*
(GOING WITH BILS.[14]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Virgins \(Jungfrauen\), or pretended Lepers.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xix^{th} chapter is about the VIRGINS. These are beggars who carry) Tj T*
(rattles as though they were real lepers, and yet they are not. This is) Tj T*
(called GOING WITH THE VIRGIN.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Mümsen, or Spurious Beggars.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xx^{th} chapter treats of the MÜMSEN. These are beggars who go) Tj T*
(about under the pretence of begging; though it is not real, like that) Tj T*
(of the Capuchin Friars who are voluntarily poor. These same men have) Tj T*
(their women sitting in out-of-the-way corners also following the) Tj T*
(business. This is called GOING WITH THE MÜMSEN.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Over-Sönzen-Goers,[15] or pretended Noblemen and Knights.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xxi^{st} chapter is about OVER-SÖNZEN-GOERS. These are vagrants or) Tj T*
(beggars who say they are of noble birth, and that they have suffered) Tj T*
