() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Veranerins, or baptized Jewesses.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xxiij^{rd} chapter is about the VERANERINS. These are women who say) Tj T*
(they are baptized Jewesses and have turned Christians, and can tell) Tj T*
(people whether their fathers or mothers are in hell or not, and beg) Tj T*
(gowns and dresses and other things, and have also false letters and) Tj T*
(seals. They are called VERANERINS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of Christianers, Calmierers, or \(pretended\) Pilgrims.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xxiiij^{th} chapter is about CHRISTIANERS or CALMIERERS. These) Tj T*
(are beggars who wear signs in their hats, especially Roman veronicas,) Tj T*
(shells, and other tokens, which they sell to each other, in order that) Tj T*
(it shall be thought they have been in distant cities and foreign parts.) Tj T*
(For this reason they wear these signs, although they have never come) Tj T*
(thence, and they deceive people thereby. They are called CALMIERERS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Seffers, or Salvers.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xxv^{th} chapter is about the SEFFERS. These are beggars who) Tj T*
(besmear themselves all over with salve, and lie down before the) Tj T*
(churches; thus looking as though they had been ill a long time, and as) Tj T*
(if their mouth and face had broken out in sores; but if they go to a) Tj T*
(bath three days after these go away again.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Schweigers, or the Jaundiced.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xxvj^{th} chapter is about the SCHWEIGERS. These are beggars who) Tj T*
(take horses? dung and mix it with water, and besmear their legs, hands,) Tj T*
(and arms with it; thereby appearing as if they had the yellow sickness,) Tj T*
(or other dreadful disease. Yet it is not true; they cheat people) Tj T*
(therewith, and they are called SCHWEIGERS.) Tj T*
