(+Of the Burkhart.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xxvij^{th} chapter is about the BURKHART. These are they who thrust) Tj T*
(their hands into gauntlets, and tie them with kerchiefs to their) Tj T*
(throats, and say they have Saint Anthony?s penance, or that of any) Tj T*
(other Saint. Yet it is not true, and they cheat people therewith. This) Tj T*
(is called GOING ON THE BURKHART.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(+Of the Platschierers, or Blind Harpers.+) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The xxviij^{th} chapter is about the PLATSCHIERERS. These are the) Tj T*
(blind men who sit before the churches on chairs, and play on the lute,) Tj T*
(and sing various songs of foreign lands whither they have never been,) Tj T*
(and when they have done singing they begin to VOP \(to lie\) and FERB) Tj T*
(in what manner they had lost their eye-sight. _Item_, the hangmen) Tj T*
(\(PLATSCHIERERS\) also before the DIFTEL door \(church-door\) will take) Tj T*
(their clothes off till they are stark-naked, and lash themselves with) Tj T*
(whips and sticks for the sake of their sins, and they do this VOPPERY) Tj T*
(to cheat mankind, as thou hast just heard in the previous chapter; and) Tj T*
(this is called PLATSCHIERING. Also those who stand on stools, and lash) Tj T*
(themselves with stones and other things, and talk about the saints,) Tj T*
(usually become hangmen and slayers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(¶ THE SECOND PART.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  _This is the Second Part of this Book, which speaketh of several_) Tj T*
(  Notabilia _that relate to the afore-mentioned customs and methods of) Tj T*
(  getting a living, given in a few words._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Item_, there are some of the afore-mentioned who neither ask before) Tj T*
(a house nor at the door, but step right into the house, or into the) Tj T*
(chamber, whether any body be within or no. It is from no good reason.) Tj T*
(These thou knowest thyself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Item_, there are also some that go up and down the aisles of churches,) Tj T*
(and carry a cup in their hands. They wear clothes suitable for this) Tj T*
(purpose, and pass about very infirm as tho? they were strangely ill,) Tj T*
(and go from one to the other, and bow towards those people who are) Tj T*
(likely to give them something. They are called PFLÜGERS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Item_, there are also some who borrow children upon All Souls? or) Tj T*
(other Feast Day, and sit down before the churches as tho? they) Tj T*
(had many children, and they say ?these children are motherless? or) Tj T*
(?fatherless,? but it is not true. This is done in order that people may) Tj T*
(give to them the more willingly for the sake of ADONE \(God\).) Tj T*
