() Tj T*
(_Exemplum_: In a village in Switzerland, there is a statute whereby) Tj T*
(they give to every beggar vs. hellers on condition that he shall for) Tj T*
(a quarter of a year at least not beg in the same neighbourhood. Once) Tj T*
(a woman took these same vs. hellers on condition that she would not) Tj T*
(beg any more in the neighbourhood. After that she cut her hair off,) Tj T*
(and begged up and down the country, and came again to Swytz, into the) Tj T*
(village, and sat down at the church gate with a young child. When the) Tj T*
(child was uncovered it was found to be a dog. Then she had to run away) Tj T*
(from the country. This person was called _Weissenburgerin_; she had) Tj T*
(been in prison at Zurich combing wool.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Item_, there are some who put on good clothes and beg in the streets.) Tj T*
(They accost any person, be it woman or man, and say, they have lain) Tj T*
(ill a long time, and are mechanics who have expended all their goods) Tj T*
(and are ashamed to beg, and ask that thou mayest help them to proceed) Tj T*
(on their journey. These are called GOOSE-SHEARERS.[16]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Item_, there are likewise some among those beforementioned who pretend) Tj T*
(they can dig or search for hidden treasures, and when they find some) Tj T*
(one who allows himself to be persuaded, they say they must have gold) Tj T*
(and silver, and must have many masses celebrated to this same end, _et) Tj T*
(cetera_, with many more words added. Thereby they deceive the nobility,) Tj T*
(the clergy, and also the laity, for it has not yet been heard that) Tj T*
(such villains have found these valuables. But they have cheated people) Tj T*
(enough. They are called SEFEL-\(dirt-\)DIGGERS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Item_, there are also some among the above who treat their children) Tj T*
(badly in order that they may become lame \(and who would be sorry if) Tj T*
(they should grow straight-legged\) for thereby they are more able to) Tj T*
(cheat people with their LOE VOTS \(lying words\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Item_, there are also others among the above who, when they come) Tj T*
(into the villages, have a little counterfeit finger and dirt[17] upon) Tj T*
(it, smearing it all over, and say they have found it, and ask if) Tj T*
(somebody will buy it. Thus a silly peasant?s wife \(HANZIN\) thinks it) Tj T*
(is silver, and knows it not, and gives them vi pennies or more for it,) Tj T*
(and therewith she is cheated. In like manner with _pater nosters_, or) Tj T*
(other signs which they carry underneath their cloaks. They are called) Tj T*
(WILTNERS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Item_, there are also some QUESTIONERERS \(persons who ask alms\)) Tj T*
(who make evil use of the holy goods which they receive, be it flax,) Tj T*
(linen-cloth, broken silver plate, or other things; they are easily) Tj T*
(detected by those who are knowing, but the common man will soon) Tj T*
(be cheated. I give to no QUESTIONER anything, excepting the four) Tj T*
(messengers, _id est_, those that are here written down, viz. _Sancti) Tj T*
(Antonii_, _Sancti Valentini_, _Sancti Bernardi_, _et Spiritus Sancti_.) Tj T*
(The same have been confirmed by the See of Rome.[18]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Item_, beware of the pedlers who seek thee at home, for thou wilt buy) Tj T*
(nothing good of them, be it silver, haberdashery, spicery, or any other) Tj T*
ET
